WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.680
No the world isn’t literally shrinking,
but for humans, it kinda is.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:10.830
Up until 500 years ago, nobody had even ever
circumnavigated the globe, but nowadays, anybody

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:14.030
can do this in a couple days just by paying
a couple thousand dollars.

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:18.541
Therefore, in the perspective of today’s
humans, the world is much smaller than it

00:00:18.541 --> 00:00:20.170
used to be.

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:24.460
In the time that it took for the first circumnavigation
of the world, today’s humans could fly to

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.570
Mars and back twice.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:32.160
Think about this, to today’s human, the
moon seems significantly closer than America

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.770
seemed to Europeans in the 16th century when
the first voyages departed.

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:41.620
It only takes humans 3 days to go the moon,
verses over two month’s for the colonists

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:43.440
to go to America.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:49.190
Up until 1693, nobody had ever been able to
circumnavigate the globe as a “tourist”,

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:52.690
as in paying for their own transit on public
transport.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:58.020
Gemelli Careri was the first person to do
this feat, and it took him 5 years.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:02.020
David Springbett has the current record for
this accomplishment with his journey from

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:09.680
Los Angeles, to London, to Bahrein, to Singapore,
to Bangkok, to Manila, to Tokyo, to Honolulu,

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.500
then back to Los Angeles in 44 hours and six
minutes.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.810
In 1800, you couldn’t even get from New
York to DC in those 44 hours.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:25.720
Between the years of 1857 and 1930, the time
it took to cross the United States went from

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.370
about three or four weeks to one day.

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:34.170
Not only has the time of travel plummeted,
the accessibility has too.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:38.280
46% of American’s will travel outside of
the US in their lifetime.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.759
Adjusted for inflation, the average cost of
domestic air travel in the US has dropped

00:01:42.759 --> 00:01:46.119
by more than 50% in just the last 30 years.

00:01:46.119 --> 00:01:55.090
50 years ago, only 20% of Americans had ever
flown in an airplane, but today, only 20%

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:56.090
haven’t.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:57.409
In some ways this may be saddening.

00:01:57.409 --> 00:01:59.030
There is less to explore.

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:03.590
There are pretty much no places left on earth
that are inaccessible to the average human

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:04.590
being.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:09.259
One can travel to every continent, every ocean,
every country with relative ease.

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:13.569
However, this does mean that practically anyone
with the thirst for adventure can visit every

00:02:13.569 --> 00:02:16.440
continent, ocean, or country.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:22.192
In the coming years, we will have even more
to explore, as we look up to the stars, and

00:02:22.192 --> 00:02:23.192
leave earth.

00:02:23.192 --> 00:02:26.410
We can already buy our tickets to go to outer
space, and although it might not feasible

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:30.980
for the everyday human to buy their ticket
now, just look how fast the price of air travel

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:31.980
plummeted.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:52.090
By the end of our lifetimes, going to the
moon might just be as easy as flying to Europe.

